l-sub">What a buyer's lender, insurer and termite inspector check on a Horry County house, the cheap fixes that help, and the projects to skip.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Fix what can stop a buyer's loan or insurance. That means a worn or leaking roof, heating or air that does not work, bare wiring, and termites or wet wood under the house.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Then do the cheap things agents recommend most, such as decluttering, cleaning and tidying the front. Skip big remodels: in a national survey, a complete kitchen remodel got back about 60 percent of its cost at sale.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina's disclosure law does not require a seller to make repairs. A buyer's lender, insurer or contract can.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Selling soon? Have us walk your house and name what a buyer's inspector will flag.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us walk your house</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#stop-a-sale" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What can stop a sale</a></li><li><a href="#how-old" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">An old roof</a></li><li><a href="#systems" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Heating, air and wiring</a></li><li><a href="#cl-100" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The CL-100</a></li><li><a href="#other-sellers" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What other sellers fix</a></li><li><a href="#cheap-fixes" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Cheap fixes that help</a></li><li><a href="#remodel" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What not to spend on</a></li><li><a href="#pre-listing" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">A pre-listing inspection</a></li><li><a href="#fix-or-credit" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Fix, credit or as is</a></li><li><a href="#repairs-before-selling-faq" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Repairs before selling FAQ</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="stop-a-sale"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What can stop a sale">Which repairs can stop a house sale in Myrtle Beach?</h2><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}.c3ph-gal{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:1.5rem;margin:1.6rem 0;padding:0;list-style:none}.c3ph-gal>figure{margin:0;min-width:0}.c3ph-gal img{aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3)}.c3ph-gal figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.45;margin-top:.5rem}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-gal{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(var(--c3ph-n,3),minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem;overflow:visible;padding:0}.c3ph-gal>figure{min-width:0}}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-gal.c3ph-row{display:flex;gap:.75rem;padding:0 var(--c3ph-bleed,1.4rem) .4rem;margin:1.6rem calc(-1*var(--c3ph-bleed,1.4rem)) 0;overflow-x:auto;scroll-snap-type:x mandatory;scroll-padding:0 var(--c3ph-bleed,1.4rem);-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;scrollbar-width:none}.c3ph-row::-webkit-scrollbar{display:none}.c3ph-row>figure{flex:0 0 82%;scroll-snap-align:start}}.c3ph-swipe{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.75rem;margin:.5rem 0 1.6rem;color:var(--navy);font:500 .95rem/1.3 var(--sans)}.c3ph-dots{display:inline-flex;gap:.45rem}.c3ph-dots i{display:block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid var(--navy);box-sizing:border-box}.c3ph-dots i:first-child{background:var(--navy)}@supports (animation-timeline:view()){.c3ph-rowbox{timeline-scope:--c3ph-p1,--c3ph-p2,--c3ph-p3}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(1){view-timeline:--c3ph-p1 inline}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(2){view-timeline:--c3ph-p2 inline}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(3){view-timeline:--c3ph-p3 inline}.c3ph-dots i{animation:c3ph-dot linear both;animation-range:cover}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(1){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p1}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(2){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p2}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(3){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p3}@keyframes c3ph-dot{0%,36%{background:transparent}42%,60%{background:var(--navy)}66%,100%{background:transparent}}}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-swipe{display:none}}.c3ph-cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.75rem;margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;padding:0;list-style:none}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-cards{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem}}@media (min-width:1000px){.c3ph-cards.c3ph-4{grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr))}}.c3ph-card{margin:0;background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.c3ph-card .c3ph-f{border-radius:0}.c3ph-card img,.c3ph-card svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3);object-fit:cover}.c3ph-card .c3ph-cr{font-size:.56rem;padding:1.2rem .45rem .25rem 1.6rem}.c3ph-card strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.98rem;font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;padding:.7rem .8rem 0}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.45;padding:.2rem .8rem .85rem}.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:720px}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The repairs that matter most are the ones a buyer's lender or insurer will not accept. Three inspections look for them: the appraisal for the buyer's loan, the insurer's four-point inspection and the termite report. Each one looks at different parts of the house:</p><div class="rp-tw"><table class="rp-tab"><caption>What a buyer's lender, insurer and termite inspector look at</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Part of the house</th><th scope="col">FHA loan appraiser</th><th scope="col">Insurer's four-point inspection</th><th scope="col">CL‑100 termite report</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Roof</th><td data-l="FHA loan appraiser">At least 2 years of life left, and no leaks. If not, a roofer must inspect it.</td><td data-l="Insurer's four-point inspection">Its age, its years of life left and the date of the last roofing permit</td><td data-l="CL‑100 termite report"><span class="no">Not checked</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Heating and air</th><td data-l="FHA loan appraiser">Heat that keeps the house at 50 degrees or more. Air conditioning, if the house has it, must work.</td><td data-l="Insurer's four-point inspection">The system's age and the year it was last updated, with a photo of its dated label</td><td data-l="CL‑100 termite report"><span class="no">Not checked</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Water heater and pipes</th><td data-l="FHA loan appraiser">A pressure-relief valve and its pipe on the water heater</td><td data-l="Insurer's four-point inspection">The water heater's age, its relief valve and any leak; the type of pipe, including polybutylene, a plastic pipe</td><td data-l="CL‑100 termite report"><span class="no">Not checked</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Wiring</th><td data-l="FHA loan appraiser">No frayed or bare wires</td><td data-l="Insurer's four-point inspection">The panel's brand, model and age, and any older kind of wiring: aluminum, cloth-covered or knob-and-tube</td><td data-l="CL‑100 termite report"><span class="no">Not checked</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Termites</th><td data-l="FHA loan appraiser">Any sign of termites, or of a past treatment, means a pest inspection</td><td data-l="Insurer's four-point inspection"><span class="no">Not checked</span></td><td data-l="CL‑100 termite report">Termites and other wood-destroying insects, and their damage</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Wood under the house</th><td data-l="FHA loan appraiser"><span class="no">Not checked</span></td><td data-l="Insurer's four-point inspection"><span class="no">Not checked</span></td><td data-l="CL‑100 termite report">Wood moisture of 20 percent or more, and rot below the main floor and at the bottoms of outside doors, steps and porch posts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The lender column uses the rules for an FHA loan, a home loan insured by the Federal Housing Administration. Its <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/default/files/Housing/documents/40001-hsgh-Update-18.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">appraiser's checklist</a> is written down in detail, so this page uses it as the example. The appraiser runs the heat, the air conditioning and the plumbing. The lender decides which repairs are required.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A buyer may use a regular loan instead. <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-1.3-06/property-condition-and-quality-construction-improvements" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae</a> is a company that buys home loans from lenders. It does not buy one on a house with damage that affects its safety, soundness or structure until the damage is fixed. A cash buyer has no lender to satisfy.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What a buyer's insurer asks</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Some insurers ask for a four-point inspection before they insure an older house. The insurer column lists the questions on <a href="https://www.citizensfla.com/inspections" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a sample four-point form from Citizens Property Insurance</a>, an insurer in Florida. An insurer here can use its own form. A wind mitigation report is a different inspection, and our <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">coastal insurance page</a> covers it.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="how-old"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="An old roof">Should you replace an old roof before selling?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not always. If it does not leak and a roofer finds at least two years of life left, an FHA loan's appraiser can accept it. A buyer's insurer may still ask how old it is. A roof that leaks, or has less life left, is the one to replace or to price into the sale.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">HUD, the federal housing department, gives an asphalt shingle roof an average life of 20 years in its table of building parts. The table was made for repair budgets on apartment buildings. It is an average. A roof can last longer or fail sooner.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Example: a house built in 2003</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About half of Horry County's homes were built in 2003 or earlier. A house built that year is 23 years old in 2026. If its parts are the ones it was built with, this is where each one stands on HUD's table:</p><ul class="rp-clock" aria-label="A house built in 2003, if its parts are the ones it was built with"><li><b>Asphalt shingle roof</b><span>HUD's life: 20 years</span><span class="past">Past it at 23</span></li><li><b>Heat pump or air conditioner, the outdoor unit</b><span>HUD's life: 15 years</span><span class="past">Past it at 23</span></li><li><b>Water heater</b><span>HUD's life: 12 years</span><span class="past">Past it at 23</span></li><li><b>Main electrical panel</b><span>HUD's life: 50 years</span><span class="ok">Not yet at 23</span></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A metal roof is in the same table at 50 years. If a part was replaced after the house was built, the age that matters is the part's age, not the house's.</p><div class="rp-side"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What to do about an old roof that does not leak</h3><ol class="rp-steps" aria-label="An old roof before you list"><li><b>Find out how old it is</b>Look for the roofer's invoice and the job's permit number. Horry County's <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/ky2jhqzz/roof-covering-letter.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">roof letter</a>, a form the owner or roofer signs in front of a notary for a roof job, also lists the permit number. The four-point form asks for the date of the last roofing permit.</li><li><b>Have a roofer look at it before you list</b>Ask for the years of life left, in writing. The four-point form asks for that number.</li><li><b>Decide once you have the roofer's letter</b>Two years or more: keep the letter with the house papers. Less than two: replace the roof, or price the house for a buyer who will.</li></ol><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-n/sell-repairs-before-selling/worn-asphalt-shingles-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-n/sell-repairs-before-selling/worn-asphalt-shingles-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-n/sell-repairs-before-selling/worn-asphalt-shingles-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Close-up of worn asphalt roof shingles with the grit worn off in patches and curled, torn edges above a gutter" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: DMahalko. License: CC BY-SA 3.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 3.0">Photo: DMahalko</a></span></div><figcaption>An asphalt shingle roof at the end of its life: the grit has worn off, and the shingle edges have curled and torn away.</figcaption></figure></div></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Not sure how old your roof is?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us what you know about it: the year, the roofer, any papers. We tell you how a buyer's inspector and insurer will see it before you list.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us check your roof's age</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="systems"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Heating, air and wiring">Should you replace the heating and air, the water heater or the wiring before selling?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Replace one before selling if it does not work, or if it is missing what the buyer's appraiser checks. Air conditioners here run more than in a northern city such as Columbus, Ohio, by <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/land-based-station/us-climate-normals" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NOAA's climate records</a>.</p><ul class="rp-plain" aria-label="What a buyer's appraiser and insurer look at, part by part"><li><b>Heat pump or air conditioner</b>HUD's table gives the outdoor unit 15 years and the indoor unit 20. The appraiser's checklist asks that the heat keep the house at 50 degrees or more. Central air is not required, but if the house has it, it must work, or the appraiser reports the cost to fix it. The four-point form wants a photo of the unit's dated label.</li><li><b>Water heater</b>HUD's table gives a home water heater 12 years. The appraiser's checklist asks for its pressure-relief valve and the pipe from it. The valve lets escaping steam or hot water out safely through the pipe.</li><li><b>Wiring and the electrical panel</b>The main panel is the metal box of breakers. HUD's table gives it 50 years, so on most houses here age is not the question. The four-point form asks the panel's brand, model and age. It also asks whether the house has any of three older kinds of wiring: aluminum, cloth-covered or knob-and-tube.</li><li><b>Aluminum wiring</b>Homes wired from 1965 to the mid-1970s <a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/s3fs-public/516.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">may have aluminum wiring</a> in the circuits to outlets and lights. About 22,000 Horry County homes were built from 1960 to 1979. A house from those years is the one to have an electrician look at before a buyer's inspector does.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A new heat pump or water heater that works is easy for a buyer to see and easy for an insurer to date. Keep the installer's invoice with the house papers.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="cl-100"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The CL-100">What is a CL‑100, and what does it check?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The CL-100 is South Carolina's termite and moisture report for a home sale or loan. It is written on the state's form, the Official South Carolina Wood Infestation Report. A buyer's FHA lender must confirm the house is free of termites, and the loan file must hold this report or a national one.</p><div class="rp-side"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Under <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/south-carolina/R-27-1085" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">state regulation 27-1085</a>, a licensed pest control inspector writes it on the state's form and signs it with their own and the company's license numbers. The inspector looks at every part of the house they can reach, including the crawlspace and the attic. They probe any damage they see and take wood moisture readings across the crawlspace.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-n/sell-repairs-before-selling/termite-damage-siding-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-n/sell-repairs-before-selling/termite-damage-siding-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-n/sell-repairs-before-selling/termite-damage-siding-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="900" alt="White painted wood siding eaten away by termites at its bottom edge, just above a block foundation and beside a screened vent" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Alton. License: CC BY-SA 3.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 3.0">Photo: Alton</a></span></div><figcaption>Termite damage in the bottom of wood siding, next to a foundation vent. A CL-100 inspector probes wood like this and reports the damage.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The report covers termites and other wood-destroying insects in every part the inspector can see and reach. It also covers rot below the main floor, plus rot in the bottoms of outside doors, door frames, steps and porch columns.</p></div><ul class="rp-plain" aria-label="What the CL-100 reports"><li><b>Wet wood under the house</b>The report flags "excessive moisture" when wood under the house reads 20 percent moisture or more, or water stands in the crawlspace. The usual fix is a plastic vapor barrier over the crawlspace soil, or more foundation vents.</li><li><b>Old damage</b>The report must list all damage, even damage left in places that were repaired before. Keep the papers from any past treatment or repair.</li><li><b>What it is not</b>It is not a warranty against termites, and it does not oblige the pest company to fix what it finds.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Older houses here are more likely to have a crawlspace. About 4 in 10 houses for sale built in 1985 or earlier list a crawlspace, against about 1 in 35 built 2006 to 2015. Most newer houses for sale here list a slab foundation, a concrete floor poured on the ground, with no crawlspace to get wet.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Got a CL-100 or an inspection report you do not understand?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send it to us. Our agents read it with you and mark what a buyer will ask you to fix.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Send us the report</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="other-sellers"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What other sellers fix">What are other sellers in Horry County fixing before they list?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Older houses for sale here advertise new roofs and new heating and air. Of the houses for sale in Horry County built in 2005 or earlier, about 1 in 6 advertise a new roof. About 1 in 8 advertise new heating and air.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Houses for sale in Horry County that advertise a new roof"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Houses for sale in Horry County that advertise a new roof</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">When built</span><span role="columnheader">advertise a new roof</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built 1985 or earlier</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.655" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">13%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built 1986 to 1995</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.815" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">16%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built 1996 to 2005</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.9" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">18%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built 2006 to 2015</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.62" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">12%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built 2016 or later</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.045" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">1%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Our count of the houses for sale on our site on November 8, 2026, not counting builders' new homes. A house counts only if its listing says so, so the true share can be higher.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We read the listing of every house for sale in Horry County on our site and counted the ones that say so. These houses compete with yours. A buyer who sees "new roof" on the house next door will ask about yours. You can <a href="/homes/map/?type=house&amp;has=new_roof" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">see the houses for sale that advertise a new roof</a> on our map.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="cheap-fixes"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Cheap fixes that help">Which cheap fixes help a house sell?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Clearing out, cleaning and tidying the front of the house help most, in <a href="https://www.nar.realtor/sites/default/files/2025-05/2025-profile-of-home-staging-05-06-2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR's 2025 national survey of agents</a>. These were the fixes agents most often recommended to sellers. NAR is the National Association of Realtors, and curb appeal is how the house looks from the street.</p><ul class="rp-pct" aria-label="Fixes agents recommend most, share of agents"><li><strong>91%</strong><span>Declutter</span></li><li><strong>88%</strong><span>Clean the whole house</span></li><li><strong>77%</strong><span>Tidy the front of the house (curb appeal)</span></li><li><strong>76%</strong><span>Professional photos</span></li><li><strong>75%</strong><span>Minor repairs</span></li><li><strong>74%</strong><span>Clean the carpets</span></li><li><strong>72%</strong><span>Put away family photos and personal things</span></li><li><strong>67%</strong><span>Touch up the paint</span></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each number is the share of agents who recommended that fix to sellers.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="remodel"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What not to spend on">Is a kitchen or bathroom remodel worth it before selling?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Usually not. In <a href="https://remodelingdoneright.nari.org/media/jjjaoome/2025remodelingimpactreport.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR's 2025 national remodeling report</a>, a complete kitchen remodel got back about 60 percent of its cost when the house sold. A bathroom remodel got back about 50 percent.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Share of a project's cost that comes back when the house sells"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Share of a project's cost that comes back when the house sells</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Project</span><span role="columnheader">cost recovered</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">New steel front door</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">100%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Complete kitchen remodel</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.6" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">60%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Minor kitchen upgrade</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.6" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">60%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Add a bathroom</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.56" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">56%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">New primary bedroom suite</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.54" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">54%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Bathroom remodel</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.5" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">50%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>NAR and the National Association of the Remodeling Industry, 2025 Remodeling Impact Report. National figures for a 2,300 square foot house in good condition.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The report's list of top projects has no row for a new roof or new heating and air, so the chart cannot show one. Agents in the same report told sellers to paint the whole house or put on a new roof more often than to upgrade the kitchen.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Which fixes are worth it on your house?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us the address. We price your house as it is and fixed up, from recorded sales near it, so you can see which fixes a buyer will pay for.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us price it as is and fixed</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="pre-listing"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="A pre-listing inspection">Should you get a pre-listing inspection before selling?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A pre-listing inspection is a home inspection you order before the house goes on the market. It is most useful on an older house, where the roof, the heating and air or the wiring may be near the end of its life. There are reasons for and against one:</p><div class="rp-two"><div><p class="k">For</p><ul><li>You learn what a buyer's inspector will find before you set the price, not in the last weeks of the sale.</li><li>You choose who does the repairs, and when.</li><li>You can price the house with the problems in mind.</li></ul></div><div><p class="k">Against</p><ul><li>The disclosure form asks what you know. Once you read the report, you know what it found.</li><li>The buyer can still order their own inspection. Under state law, the buyer still has the duty to inspect.</li><li>It costs money before you have a buyer.</li></ul></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our page on the <a href="/sell/seller-disclosure/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">seller disclosure form</a> covers what happens when you attach a report to the form, and goes through the form question by question. A South Carolina real estate attorney can tell you how the disclosure law treats a report you paid for.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="fix-or-credit"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Fix, credit or as is">Fix it, give a credit, or sell as is?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Before you list, and again after the buyer's inspection, you have three choices on each problem:</p><ul class="rp-cards" aria-label="Fix it, give a credit, or sell as is"><li><span class="k">The buyer's loan requires it</span><b>Fix it</b>The lender can require a repair before closing. It can be a roof the lender will not accept, or damage to the structure.</li><li><span class="k">Help with costs</span><b>Give a credit</b>You pay toward the buyer's costs, and the buyer makes the repair after closing. The buyer's loan limits how much a seller can pay toward those costs.</li><li><span class="k">You will not fix it</span><b>Sell as is</b>Under South Carolina's disclosure law, buyer and seller can agree to an as-is sale. You still fill in the disclosure form. Our <a href="/sell/cash-offer/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">cash offer page</a> covers selling as is to an investor.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">To choose between a fix and a credit, get a written price for the fix and compare it with what the buyer asks for. This page gives no repair prices. They change with the house and the company, and we found no public source for prices here.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If something changes after you hand over the disclosure form, such as a new leak, state law says you correct the form or make reasonable repairs before closing.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our <a href="/sell/net-proceeds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">net proceeds calculator</a> shows what a credit or a lower price leaves you.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="repairs-before-selling-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Repairs before selling FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who pays for the CL-100 in South Carolina, the buyer or the seller?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The state regulation for the report does not say who orders it or who pays for it. The buyer and seller agree on that in the sale contract. A buyer's lender can also ask for one before it lends.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How old can a roof be for insurance in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We found no roof age limit in South Carolina's insurance laws. Insurers can still ask about the roof. The sample four-point form from Citizens, a Florida insurer, asks for the roof's age and its years of life left.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How long is a CL-100 good for?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A CL-100 has no expiry date in the state regulation. A buyer's lender or the sale contract can ask for a report done within a set number of days.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do I have to fix what the buyer's inspector finds?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not under South Carolina's disclosure law. You fix what you agree to fix in the sale contract, and a buyer's loan can still require some repairs before it closes.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Are repairs before selling tax deductible?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The IRS treats painting and fixing leaks as repairs. Repairs do not go into your home's basis, what you paid plus improvements, unless they are part of a larger remodeling job. A new roof is an improvement and does. A tax professional can tell you how this applies to your sale.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/default/files/Housing/documents/40001-hsgh-Update-18.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD Handbook 4000.1</a>, <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/documents/eul_for_cna_e_tool.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD useful life table</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-1.3-06/property-condition-and-quality-construction-improvements" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-1.3-06</a>, <a href="https://www.citizensfla.com/inspections" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Citizens inspections</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/ky2jhqzz/roof-covering-letter.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County roof letter</a>, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/south-carolina/R-27-1085" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Reg. 27-1085</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=27&chapter=50" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/recpdf/Property-Condition-Disclosure-Statement-06.2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC disclosure form</a>, <a href="https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT1Y2025.B25034?g=050XX00US45051" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census ACS 2025</a>, <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/land-based-station/us-climate-normals" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NOAA climate normals</a>, <a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/s3fs-public/516.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CPSC Pub. 516</a>, <a href="https://www.nar.realtor/sites/default/files/2025-05/2025-profile-of-home-staging-05-06-2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR 2025 staging</a>, <a href="https://remodelingdoneright.nari.org/media/jjjaoome/2025remodelingimpactreport.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR and NARI 2025</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p523" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Pub. 523</a>. Houses for sale counted from the listings on our site, Horry County, November 8, 2026, in aggregate. Home ages: Census ACS 2025 1-year, all homes.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We are not attorneys, inspectors or tax advisers, and nothing on this page is legal or tax advice. For advice on your own sale, talk to a South Carolina attorney or a tax professional.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">An asphalt shingle roof at the end of its life: the grit has worn off, and the shingle edges have curled and torn away. Photo: DMahalko (Dale Mahalko), <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Failure_of_asphalt_shingles_allowing_roof_leakage.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty resized the photo, and cropped 16:9 and square copies for sharing.</div><div role="listitem">Termite damage in the bottom of wood siding, 